From 6637e92c6e868b17ddd437b357b579a9f9afd689 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 05:56:19 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(sim): the hole-wall excuse measures a facet about its
 hole's axis, so a tool cutting into a flat is a gouge

The containment kinds' (drill, bore, thread mill, cross drill) solid-law
excuse read a facet by its corners against the tool's own disc: any facet
whose vertices stood outside the tool was forgiven its edges' dip into
it, a bound that grows as the tool shrinks against the hole. A flat whose
corners lie on the hole circle - an octagon, a D-bore, a square - passes
inside it along its middle, and a cut there went unconvicted (a thread
mill 0.32 mm into an octagon's flats, a bore 0.38 mm: 0 warnings).

The operation now names its holes' axes (verify::solid_hits_walled,
HoleAxis), and a facet is excused only when it is a round wall of one of
them: its sagitta about that axis - nearest vertex radius less its
nearest approach (point-to-segment / point-in-triangle, square to the
axis) - is at most WALL_SAG (0.05 mm). The excuse is the smaller of that
sagitta and the old edge-dip bound, so the law never convicts less than
before. Non-finite facets, facets over the axis, axes not along the
move's tool axis, and laws with no holes named excuse nothing.

Fixes #1205

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
